WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:04.629
Just the other day,
the partners at my firm pulled me aside

00:00:05.088 --> 00:00:08.091
and they were visibly upset

00:00:08.258 --> 00:00:10.301
about something that I had done.

00:00:11.386 --> 00:00:13.722
A portion of this video
sponsored by Vessey.

00:00:14.055 --> 00:00:16.433
More on them later in this video.

00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:19.894
You made a decision
that we can all agree on

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.564
and you were not being transparent
about this.

00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:27.402
Honestly,
I did speak to everyone individually.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:28.278
Hey, guys.

00:00:28.278 --> 00:00:31.990
My name's Dami, and I'm an architect
in beautiful Vancouver, B.C..

00:00:32.073 --> 00:00:36.036
When I got that feedback, I cried.

00:00:36.244 --> 00:00:37.579
No, I'm just kidding.

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:40.040
I had a lot of bad emotions.

00:00:40.582 --> 00:00:44.294
You know, I had done my version
of what I thought was right.

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:48.423
And it really felt
like I was being accused

00:00:48.423 --> 00:00:53.386
of being a bad architect
and even a dishonest person.

00:00:53.511 --> 00:00:56.431
As I kind of progress into my career.

00:00:56.598 --> 00:00:59.267
And also,
as I kind of built my YouTube business,

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:04.606
I've been getting criticism of all kinds,
including ones like this.

00:01:04.773 --> 00:01:07.108
And it's
been really challenging to manage.

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:11.112
So lately I've been learning a lot about

00:01:11.321 --> 00:01:15.283
how to deal with criticism
and feedback more effectively.

00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:18.244
So today I want to talk about some of
the things that I learned from the book.

00:01:18.453 --> 00:01:20.080
Thanks for the feedback.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.957
In the first part of the video,
I'm going to talk about how to receive

00:01:23.041 --> 00:01:24.292
that feedback.

00:01:24.292 --> 00:01:28.546
And the second part of the video, I'm
going to talk about how to navigate that

00:01:28.546 --> 00:01:32.675
feedback conversation and how to implement
that feedback into your lives.

00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:35.804
And in the atrium, this is where you have

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:39.099
your communal gatherings.

00:01:39.641 --> 00:01:41.976
It's a pretty model, but

00:01:43.228 --> 00:01:44.687
I don't think you're quite

00:01:44.687 --> 00:01:47.982
capturing the essence of the library

00:01:48.316 --> 00:01:51.736
as an institution, given
the ontology of the library.

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:57.033
You know, a library is an institution
of culture and history.

00:01:57.784 --> 00:02:03.998
And you expect people
to just sit around here like that.

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:08.002
Handling
feedback on your project is one thing

00:02:08.002 --> 00:02:12.757
and handling feedback on your performance
or your voice.

00:02:13.508 --> 00:02:15.093
That's a whole nother thing.

00:02:15.093 --> 00:02:18.930
And in fact,
it is really important for you

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:22.016
to categorize
the different types of criticism.

00:02:22.183 --> 00:02:24.102
So there's three types of feedback.

00:02:24.102 --> 00:02:26.146
There's evaluation.

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:29.065
That's
where you talk about where you stand

00:02:29.065 --> 00:02:33.194
in relation to the expectations
and to other people.

00:02:33.403 --> 00:02:35.947
There's coaching or advice,

00:02:36.364 --> 00:02:38.950
which is all about helping you improve,

00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:42.162
and I think a crit or like a performance

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:45.373
review is probably somewhere
in between those two.

00:02:45.623 --> 00:02:49.002
There's also appreciation or recognition

00:02:49.002 --> 00:02:52.005
or things which basically lets

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:56.467
you know that you're doing well
and that your efforts are being noticed.

00:02:56.593 --> 00:03:01.598
So when you're asking for feedback,
you actually want to be fairly clear

00:03:01.598 --> 00:03:07.896
on what you're looking for just to prevent
confusion or conflict from both parties.

00:03:08.146 --> 00:03:13.693
The hardest part about receiving feedback
is actually the emotional response,

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:19.115
and it's the emotional response
which makes us angry or defensive.

00:03:19.449 --> 00:03:24.078
And a lot of the times
we can get dismissive of that feedback.

00:03:24.078 --> 00:03:28.458
But if you identify what triggers
those emotional responses,

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:32.545
you can have much better control
over those reactions.

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:35.048
Dealing with criticism
can take a toll on you.

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:38.801
For me, whenever I feel overwhelmed,
I like to go outside

00:03:38.801 --> 00:03:40.720
and spend some time in nature.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:45.391
But sometimes we don't always have dry
weather or dry land.

