00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.029
Generally there's three types of triggers.

00:04:40.029 --> 00:04:41.906
There's the truth triggers.

00:04:41.906 --> 00:04:44.867
So truth triggers
our responses to feedback

00:04:44.867 --> 00:04:49.539
we feel is wrong or unhelpful or unfair.

00:04:49.664 --> 00:04:54.419
But as we know,
everyone has their own idea of the truth.

00:04:54.585 --> 00:04:58.840
So the very, very
first thing you want to do is instead of

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:03.886
thinking, No, that's wrong, or, Oh, well,
that makes sense for you, but not for me.

00:05:04.053 --> 00:05:09.100
You have to acknowledge that everyone
thinks differently, and even if it's hard,

00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:13.938
try to ask yourself like what is right
about what the other person's saying.

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:19.444
At the same time, try to examine yourself
for any blindspots in your thinking.

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:21.654
There's behavior patterns.

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:25.575
So we often have behaviors

00:05:25.742 --> 00:05:29.412
that are totally unknown to us.

00:05:29.412 --> 00:05:31.748
But others B.S. it really clearly.

00:05:31.748 --> 00:05:34.876
A really good example is using filler
words like

00:05:34.876 --> 00:05:38.129
ums or Oz or like

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:41.799
like that,

00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:45.136
because they've been so embedded

00:05:45.136 --> 00:05:49.432
into your speech pattern that you don't
even recognize how you do it.

00:05:49.474 --> 00:05:52.560
And for me,
I only recognize that I do it a lot.

00:05:52.602 --> 00:05:58.191
When I started making videos on YouTube
and another blind spot is typically like

00:05:58.566 --> 00:06:03.613
you judged your actions
by the intent and others

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:09.077
and judge your actions on impact
and especially how it impacts them.

00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:15.041
And paradoxically,
when we come into challenging situations,

00:06:15.249 --> 00:06:18.503
we tend to attribute it
to the circumstance.

00:06:18.586 --> 00:06:22.382
But other people tend
to connect it to our character.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:25.385
So it's come to my attention by you

00:06:25.385 --> 00:06:28.388
made a decision
on the glazing frame colors

00:06:28.513 --> 00:06:33.851
that we can all agree on, and you were not
being transparent about this.

00:06:34.185 --> 00:06:37.397
And if this keeps happening,
this is the fastest way

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:39.482
we're going to lose trust in you.

00:06:39.482 --> 00:06:41.067
I had to make that call.

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:44.445
I was already so late
on getting back to them and I couldn't

00:06:44.445 --> 00:06:49.409
wait another week for us to all meet
and honestly,

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:53.913
I did speak to everyone individually
and my understanding

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:57.417
from the conversation was that
we were all aligned on picking white.

00:06:57.458 --> 00:07:02.130
We also have tells
that we might not be aware of like

00:07:02.672 --> 00:07:05.258
facial expressions or voice

00:07:05.675 --> 00:07:10.680
and any other,
like nonverbal behaviors or like habits

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:14.809
that can say the opposite thing
of your intentions.

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:20.022
There's also emotional distortion
when our emotional reaction to something

00:07:20.022 --> 00:07:24.277
is like way out of proportion
to the content of the feedback.

00:07:24.485 --> 00:07:27.905
You can find some of these blind spots
by observing

00:07:27.905 --> 00:07:31.659
how you get really defensive
or recording yourself.

00:07:31.993 --> 00:07:36.247
You can also look for patterns
in the feedback, like if you're getting

00:07:36.247 --> 00:07:39.917
the same feedback from different people,
you know that's an issue.

00:07:40.042 --> 00:07:44.046
And the second type of trigger
is relationship triggers, which is

00:07:44.213 --> 00:07:49.302
an emotional response to the person
more than the content of the feedback.

00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:53.055
So there's two main types of relationship
triggers.

00:07:53.097 --> 00:07:56.225
One is our opinion of that person.

00:07:56.684 --> 00:08:01.397
So when we get negative feedback,
we are subconsciously

00:08:01.397 --> 00:08:06.110
looking for ways to disqualify that person
from giving that feedback.

00:08:06.152 --> 00:08:08.905
And then number two is how they treat us.

00:08:09.363 --> 00:08:13.242
So if you feel like
they're not appreciating you

00:08:13.367 --> 00:08:17.455
or your efforts or you feel like
they don't have the authority

00:08:17.538 --> 00:08:20.666
or you you might just feel like that
person doesn't really like you.

00:08:20.708 --> 00:08:24.754
And in situations
like this, a very common response

00:08:25.129 --> 00:08:29.008
can be for us to respond with something
that's like

00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:32.803
aimed at the person
rather than the issue itself.

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:37.850
And when this happens,
it will split the conversation into.

00:08:37.934 --> 00:08:42.188
And you just start talking
about two completely different things.

00:08:42.271 --> 00:08:46.442
So, for example, your roommate will say,
oh, you're so messy

00:08:46.859 --> 00:08:50.238
and you'll say something like, Oh, yeah,
but you're so loud.

00:08:50.238 --> 00:08:52.823
Like, you leave the TV on till 2 a.m.

00:08:52.823 --> 00:08:53.991
and I can never sleep.

00:08:53.991 --> 00:08:59.830
And so it's very important for you
to recognize this and actually address

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:04.335
each one of these topics separately
before they turn into bigger problems.

00:09:04.377 --> 00:09:08.589
And sometimes the conflict
might not even be happening

00:09:08.589 --> 00:09:11.384
because of you or the other person,

00:09:11.717 --> 00:09:15.346
but because we're all a part of the system

00:09:15.346 --> 00:09:17.640
that defines our relationship.

00:09:18.307 --> 00:09:21.477
And you might have different
values or habits

00:09:21.477 --> 00:09:24.605
and you might have different roles
in the relationship.

00:09:24.855 --> 00:09:27.483
So sometimes just like stepping back

00:09:27.733 --> 00:09:32.280
and like looking at the whole system
as a whole can be really helpful.

00:09:32.321 --> 00:09:35.199
The next one is identity triggers,

00:09:36.075 --> 00:09:38.119
and these ones are a big one.

00:09:38.286 --> 00:09:41.581
Identity triggers can happen
when the feedback doesn't align

00:09:41.581 --> 00:09:46.544
with your sense of who you are
or the story that you tell about yourself.

00:09:46.586 --> 00:09:49.880
For me, like when I got that feedback
from my bosses,

00:09:50.381 --> 00:09:53.676
a part of the reason why it stung so much

00:09:53.676 --> 00:10:02.810
was because it was an identity trigger.

00:10:04.895 --> 00:10:08.441
I felt
like it was an attack on my integrity

00:10:08.733 --> 00:10:12.486
and my capability as a person
and as an architect.

00:10:12.486 --> 00:10:16.365
And, you know, I consider myself
to be a very transparent person.

00:10:16.532 --> 00:10:19.619
And that's kind of one of the things
that I take a lot of pride in.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:24.206
And so my emotional response
was probably kind

00:10:24.206 --> 00:10:28.836
of exaggerated
compared to the issue at hand.

00:10:28.961 --> 00:10:32.340
And I think the best way to deal with
identity triggers

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:35.384
is just like adopting a growth mindset.

00:10:35.509 --> 00:10:38.721
A person with a growth mindset
sees their traits

00:10:38.721 --> 00:11:11.504
as evolving.

00:11:12.588 --> 00:11:15.758
But if you have a fixed mindset,

00:11:15.883 --> 00:11:18.969
you will see your abilities as stable

00:11:19.303 --> 00:11:27.937
and finished.

00:11:27.937 --> 00:11:31.065
And there's actually a ways
you can develop a growth mindset

00:11:31.065 --> 00:11:36.445
by listening to feedback as coaching
rather than evaluation or judgment.

00:11:36.529 --> 00:11:39.573
It's more about like
how you're doing things

00:11:39.573 --> 00:11:42.660
and what you're doing rather than on you.

00:11:42.785 --> 00:11:46.831
Also, don't worry so much about how
this is affecting the other person's

00:11:46.831 --> 00:11:47.748
opinion of you.

00:11:47.748 --> 00:11:52.795
You know, in the end, it's really hard
to change people's opinion of you,

00:11:53.254 --> 00:11:57.883
but it's really easy for you to change
how you reflect on yourself.

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:00.136
So just try to focus on that.

00:12:00.177 --> 00:12:02.346
Also, whenever you get feedback,

00:12:02.596 --> 00:12:06.183
try to evaluate yourself
on how you're taking that feedback.

00:12:06.225 --> 00:12:10.187
Like if you can handle that feedback
better than others,

00:12:10.312 --> 00:12:14.567
then appreciate yourself for that
because that in itself

00:12:14.567 --> 00:12:18.362
is a really positive trait
that actually very few people have.

00:12:18.404 --> 00:12:21.407
Identity triggers are probably one

00:12:21.407 --> 00:12:24.118
of the most emotional out of all of these.

00:12:24.535 --> 00:12:28.414
So try to monitor your emotional reactions
thoughtfully

00:12:28.456 --> 00:12:31.751
by trying to control your emotions,

00:12:31.751 --> 00:12:35.796
or even better, like trying
to separate your emotions from the facts.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:40.676
I also have my own kind
of strange strategy for this.

00:12:40.843 --> 00:12:45.181
So whenever I'm in a bad situation
or I get bad feedback,

00:12:45.181 --> 00:12:49.393
I imagine myself as like a character
in a comedy movie

00:12:49.643 --> 00:12:53.731
or in a sitcom,

00:12:53.731 --> 00:12:58.486
and I just kind of proceed
to deal with the issue at hand.

00:12:58.694 --> 00:13:02.114
Yes, it is important
to adopt a growth mindset.

00:13:02.531 --> 00:13:05.826
But there are certain things
that are just like

00:13:05.826 --> 00:13:09.997
outside of your control,
like how others feel about you,

00:13:10.122 --> 00:13:14.460
and especially after you do your best
to remedy the situation.

00:13:14.543 --> 00:13:18.339
If they still have a negative
feeling towards you,

00:13:18.756 --> 00:13:23.260
just let it go
and just try to be sad about it

00:13:23.803 --> 00:13:26.806
because it's a waste of your time
and your energy

00:13:26.806 --> 00:13:30.100
to keep thinking about things
that are outside of your control.

00:13:30.392 --> 00:13:33.479
Now that we've identified the triggers
and we kind of understand

00:13:33.479 --> 00:13:34.605
how to control them,

00:13:34.605 --> 00:13:39.193
how do we navigate those conversations
and get the most out of that feedback?

00:13:39.276 --> 00:13:42.696
Douglas Stone defines
three phases of a feedback

00:13:42.696 --> 00:13:46.408
conversation
the open, the body and the close.

00:13:46.575 --> 00:13:50.454
The open is before the content
of the feedback.

00:13:50.621 --> 00:13:54.291
It's where you get aligned
with the other person

00:13:54.667 --> 00:13:58.671
by trying to understand
what kind of feedback is this

00:13:59.004 --> 00:14:01.674
and what's the intent behind
this feedback?

00:14:01.715 --> 00:14:04.844
Is this evaluation? Is this coaching?

00:14:05.135 --> 00:14:06.762
Is this appreciation?

00:14:06.762 --> 00:14:09.056
And also trying to understand

00:14:09.306 --> 00:14:13.227
who has the final word
and is this feedback negotiable?

00:14:13.310 --> 00:14:18.983
If you're working for someone,
your boss in most cases has a final word.

00:14:19.775 --> 00:14:23.362
No matter how right you think you are.

00:14:23.571 --> 00:14:26.407
In the end,
it does kind of come down to this.

00:14:26.782 --> 00:14:28.617
You are there by choice.

00:14:28.617 --> 00:14:30.661
You know, you agreed to the deal.

00:14:30.744 --> 00:14:35.833
You work for them and they are paying you
to represent the firm.

00:14:35.958 --> 00:14:39.003
And obviously,
there's a lot of nuance to this,

00:14:39.003 --> 00:14:43.966
but that's the kind of systems framework
that you have to consider.

00:14:44.049 --> 00:14:46.051
And then there's the body.

00:14:46.051 --> 00:14:51.682
The body is the content of the feedback
or like the meat of the talk.

00:14:51.807 --> 00:14:55.436
And to get the most out of this feedback,
you need to listen.

00:14:55.728 --> 00:14:58.272
That's something that I'm really,
really trying to work on.

00:14:58.355 --> 00:15:02.860
Try to really pay attention
to what the other person's saying.

00:15:03.277 --> 00:15:08.324
And while you're doing this,
listen to your own internal voice

00:15:08.324 --> 00:15:11.243
and try to watch for the triggers
that we talked about.

00:15:11.535 --> 00:15:15.915
And then clarify,
you can always ask for specificities

00:15:16.123 --> 00:15:20.544
or specifics
on expectations or consequence.

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:26.050
And you can also add your own input
and try to explain the situation

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:29.637
in your own thoughts
and in your observations.

00:15:29.678 --> 00:15:32.848
You can also talk
about your interpretations on things

00:15:32.848 --> 00:15:33.891
or your feelings.

00:15:33.891 --> 00:15:36.977
You can also try to referee
the conversation.

00:15:37.436 --> 00:15:40.814
Try to diagnose and describe

00:15:40.814 --> 00:15:43.484
the communication problems as they come up

00:15:43.817 --> 00:15:47.780
so that you can kind of propose solutions
in a real time.

00:15:47.863 --> 00:15:51.367
And the last stage of that is the clothes.

00:15:51.575 --> 00:15:56.830
That's where you clarify the commitments
and expectations and the follow up.

00:15:56.830 --> 00:16:01.418
You know who's going to do what
specifically when you leave the meeting?

00:16:01.585 --> 00:16:06.674
Are there like benchmarks or consequences
if you don't meet those benchmarks?

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:09.301
And are you going to be having
regular check ins?

00:16:09.551 --> 00:16:15.432
Then the last but the most important step
is actually incorporating the feedback.

00:16:15.557 --> 00:16:19.853
Try to focus on one thing at a time or,
you know, tested it

00:16:19.853 --> 00:16:23.524
with small experiments and make sure

00:16:23.524 --> 00:16:26.902
you really, really understand
what the other person's trying to see.

00:16:27.152 --> 00:16:30.364
You can give yourself rewards
for making these changes,

00:16:30.364 --> 00:16:34.118
or you can also add consequences
for not making these changes.

00:16:34.243 --> 00:16:38.622
Also, try to make the other person
feel valued by taking their advice

00:16:39.039 --> 00:16:42.876
and later,
they are going to come to you for advice.

00:16:43.043 --> 00:16:44.336
In conclusion.

00:16:44.336 --> 00:16:48.549
What's interesting,
but also really true, is that

00:16:48.674 --> 00:16:51.927
negative feedback,
if you know how to handle it,

00:16:52.636 --> 00:16:55.014
it can actually have a much more positive

00:16:55.014 --> 00:16:58.267
impact on your life than positive impact.

00:16:58.392 --> 00:17:00.561
Think about an architecture project.

00:17:00.561 --> 00:17:04.690
If you get really, really positive
feedback, then at the end of the day,

00:17:05.107 --> 00:17:07.860
it's kind of hard
to know where to take that.

00:17:07.985 --> 00:17:11.447
But when you have a really bad crit,
you might cry.

00:17:11.655 --> 00:17:16.035
But you have so many different ways
you can take your project afterwards.

00:17:16.035 --> 00:17:16.535
Right?

00:17:16.535 --> 00:17:19.663
So try to really use this feedback

00:17:19.747 --> 00:17:23.083
to your advantage
and really make something of it.

00:17:23.208 --> 00:17:25.544
